[[:db/retract 17592186119102 :answer-field/choices 17592186119105] [:db/retract 17592186119102 :answer-field/choices 17592186119106] [:db/retract 17592186119102 :answer-field/choices 17592186119107] [:db/retract 17592186119102 :answer-field/choices 17592186119103] {:db/id 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6116bb62-b350-5926-8756-9fc43f77db50"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-21c086f0-0661-5c6d-8a42-5fe15673cf33" "question-455e62c8-3b81-5de7-9ef0-5fb64015c1fb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-577c86ab-d1fb-551c-9998-1ea74c39e37e" "question-8e82dd54-dc76-5073-8b6d-fd7d44dc815e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120322 :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,j-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})\\,k] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}+(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+9{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+25{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,}=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186118071 :answer-field/correct 17592186118075 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-6116bb62-b350-5926-8756-9fc43f77db50" :question/id #uuid "6116bb62-b350-5926-8756-9fc43f77db50" :question/math-academy-id "q-132107" :question/problem "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{8}))}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{16})}=16.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40148947-347b-5613-b0f0-048b08f6395d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40148947-347b-5613-b0f0-048b08f6395d" :answer-field/id #uuid "40148947-347b-5613-b0f0-048b08f6395d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bec816be-742c-59d2-855d-77a5f733bd12" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bec816be-742c-59d2-855d-77a5f733bd12" "answer-b1ee4cdf-998e-5716-aef1-4bb01bec2434" "answer-60712d5b-cdb0-55d3-a4f8-50942ccd11f8" "answer-bac1618d-71c7-5ccf-9a5d-6130f69bfae6" "answer-c3741ba0-1c15-5491-b929-38f45abef427"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bec816be-742c-59d2-855d-77a5f733bd12" :answer/id #uuid "bec816be-742c-59d2-855d-77a5f733bd12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1ee4cdf-998e-5716-aef1-4bb01bec2434" :answer/id #uuid "b1ee4cdf-998e-5716-aef1-4bb01bec2434" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60712d5b-cdb0-55d3-a4f8-50942ccd11f8" :answer/id #uuid "60712d5b-cdb0-55d3-a4f8-50942ccd11f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bac1618d-71c7-5ccf-9a5d-6130f69bfae6" :answer/id #uuid "bac1618d-71c7-5ccf-9a5d-6130f69bfae6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3741ba0-1c15-5491-b929-38f45abef427" :answer/id #uuid "c3741ba0-1c15-5491-b929-38f45abef427" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-21c086f0-0661-5c6d-8a42-5fe15673cf33" :question/id #uuid "21c086f0-0661-5c6d-8a42-5fe15673cf33" :question/math-academy-id "q-97706" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,1)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is \n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,1⟩,$ since the point $P(1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(1+4{t}^{2})^{3}}},\\,-\\frac{4t}{\\sqrt{(1+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(1+4(0)^{2})^{3}}},\\,-\\frac{4(0)}{\\sqrt{(1+4(0)^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨2,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,0⟩=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,0⟩=⟨\\frac{3}{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f1ed9b22-e11c-5770-9922-e0f3dca90fb8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1ed9b22-e11c-5770-9922-e0f3dca90fb8" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ed9b22-e11c-5770-9922-e0f3dca90fb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a7e95d1-a185-5c36-941f-f42bb0377bd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52439b04-8154-53ed-8e04-90bf6ccb85dc" "answer-0e7c676a-8ada-5b80-8687-e725bb845066" "answer-160ecfc6-2535-55df-b403-21e3404605cd" "answer-5bb242ab-2a10-5e82-bf8e-855144702e18" "answer-3a7e95d1-a185-5c36-941f-f42bb0377bd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52439b04-8154-53ed-8e04-90bf6ccb85dc" :answer/id #uuid "52439b04-8154-53ed-8e04-90bf6ccb85dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(-1,1)$ and the radius $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e7c676a-8ada-5b80-8687-e725bb845066" :answer/id #uuid "0e7c676a-8ada-5b80-8687-e725bb845066" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(2,0)$ and the radius $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-160ecfc6-2535-55df-b403-21e3404605cd" :answer/id #uuid "160ecfc6-2535-55df-b403-21e3404605cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(3,1)$ and the radius $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bb242ab-2a10-5e82-bf8e-855144702e18" :answer/id #uuid "5bb242ab-2a10-5e82-bf8e-855144702e18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a7e95d1-a185-5c36-941f-f42bb0377bd0" :answer/id #uuid "3a7e95d1-a185-5c36-941f-f42bb0377bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(\\frac{3}{2},1)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-455e62c8-3b81-5de7-9ef0-5fb64015c1fb" :question/id #uuid "455e62c8-3b81-5de7-9ef0-5fb64015c1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-132206" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3})$ where $t=1,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\sqrt{\\frac{t}{t+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(1)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩,$ since the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3})$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{\\frac{t}{t+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}(t+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2(t+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}(1+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{1}{2(1+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2(2)^{3/2}},-\\frac{1}{2(2)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=1$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)+R(1)N(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩+4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩+4⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3}⟩+⟨4,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{10}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a97de606-d3e0-57f4-9214-46ea151db54d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a97de606-d3e0-57f4-9214-46ea151db54d" :answer-field/id #uuid "a97de606-d3e0-57f4-9214-46ea151db54d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-935d3fcb-4001-59af-9ebd-2e9f785033b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84c1582a-9c55-52d8-bdb8-f1855dfee339" "answer-56d2d7f8-0ae6-569f-883d-0ab6f7d76ff9" "answer-b7dff845-3395-5778-ba65-c9c3d11732df" "answer-343bc97a-3f6b-52b9-83d8-ee3bcc396c7c" "answer-935d3fcb-4001-59af-9ebd-2e9f785033b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84c1582a-9c55-52d8-bdb8-f1855dfee339" :answer/id #uuid "84c1582a-9c55-52d8-bdb8-f1855dfee339" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $⟨-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56d2d7f8-0ae6-569f-883d-0ab6f7d76ff9" :answer/id #uuid "56d2d7f8-0ae6-569f-883d-0ab6f7d76ff9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $⟨-\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{5}{9}⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7dff845-3395-5778-ba65-c9c3d11732df" :answer/id #uuid "b7dff845-3395-5778-ba65-c9c3d11732df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $⟨-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{5}{9}⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-343bc97a-3f6b-52b9-83d8-ee3bcc396c7c" :answer/id #uuid "343bc97a-3f6b-52b9-83d8-ee3bcc396c7c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{5}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-935d3fcb-4001-59af-9ebd-2e9f785033b6" :answer/id #uuid "935d3fcb-4001-59af-9ebd-2e9f785033b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $⟨\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{10}{3}⟩$ and the radius is $4\\sqrt{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120498 :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,1⟩,$ since the point $P(0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2}+1)^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,1⟩+1⟨0,1⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119102 :answer-field/correct "answer-c21a2526-c8f2-5cf5-8dba-ef6702413f42" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d94db2da-7382-5233-8016-048bd89a7acd" "answer-c21a2526-c8f2-5cf5-8dba-ef6702413f42" "answer-18864349-f844-598b-8bd8-2499081b262c" "answer-26713c2f-51c0-5113-aace-e97dae2a9d84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d94db2da-7382-5233-8016-048bd89a7acd" :answer/id #uuid "d94db2da-7382-5233-8016-048bd89a7acd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c21a2526-c8f2-5cf5-8dba-ef6702413f42" :answer/id #uuid "c21a2526-c8f2-5cf5-8dba-ef6702413f42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(0,2)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18864349-f844-598b-8bd8-2499081b262c" :answer/id #uuid "18864349-f844-598b-8bd8-2499081b262c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(1,0)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26713c2f-51c0-5113-aace-e97dae2a9d84" :answer/id #uuid "26713c2f-51c0-5113-aace-e97dae2a9d84" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-577c86ab-d1fb-551c-9998-1ea74c39e37e" :question/id #uuid "577c86ab-d1fb-551c-9998-1ea74c39e37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132119" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=2$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(2),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(2)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(2)$ and ${r}^{″}(2)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(2) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(2){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(2)\\times {r}^{″}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}})}^{3}}{(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{27}{16\\sqrt{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4dfff41f-ad79-57e1-bbe0-18110bfed6e2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dfff41f-ad79-57e1-bbe0-18110bfed6e2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dfff41f-ad79-57e1-bbe0-18110bfed6e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbe72f53-9ff7-54c2-8de2-57f21faf388d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fbe72f53-9ff7-54c2-8de2-57f21faf388d" "answer-17dfe97c-105a-5dbd-a5d8-f67bff74a3eb" "answer-bf6dafae-a662-56d5-adac-629f556d6830" "answer-c8aa7bd2-92e2-5691-ad30-ce9f13f06fb4" "answer-daaccc09-6276-5373-8cc7-35d3a431e7a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbe72f53-9ff7-54c2-8de2-57f21faf388d" :answer/id #uuid "fbe72f53-9ff7-54c2-8de2-57f21faf388d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17dfe97c-105a-5dbd-a5d8-f67bff74a3eb" :answer/id #uuid "17dfe97c-105a-5dbd-a5d8-f67bff74a3eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf6dafae-a662-56d5-adac-629f556d6830" :answer/id #uuid "bf6dafae-a662-56d5-adac-629f556d6830" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8aa7bd2-92e2-5691-ad30-ce9f13f06fb4" :answer/id #uuid "c8aa7bd2-92e2-5691-ad30-ce9f13f06fb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daaccc09-6276-5373-8cc7-35d3a431e7a1" :answer/id #uuid "daaccc09-6276-5373-8cc7-35d3a431e7a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8e82dd54-dc76-5073-8b6d-fd7d44dc815e" :question/id #uuid "8e82dd54-dc76-5073-8b6d-fd7d44dc815e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98717" :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨0,-4,0⟩\\times ⟨-4,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-16\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{{4}^{3}}{\\,16\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3fbeb716-f779-5cae-a88d-6239351ca62c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3fbeb716-f779-5cae-a88d-6239351ca62c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fbeb716-f779-5cae-a88d-6239351ca62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-63e39650-4998-587f-bb67-a3e3b9b1823e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d346ea8-2679-5e9c-813d-0909d9f6e1d4" "answer-fee16127-fffd-5793-b350-764f5e703dc6" "answer-63e39650-4998-587f-bb67-a3e3b9b1823e" "answer-997d42bb-b84a-5f7f-8a34-f6035e4d200f" "answer-2224ac84-3f36-51f0-b57f-7543c32e2f98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d346ea8-2679-5e9c-813d-0909d9f6e1d4" :answer/id #uuid "9d346ea8-2679-5e9c-813d-0909d9f6e1d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fee16127-fffd-5793-b350-764f5e703dc6" :answer/id #uuid "fee16127-fffd-5793-b350-764f5e703dc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63e39650-4998-587f-bb67-a3e3b9b1823e" :answer/id #uuid "63e39650-4998-587f-bb67-a3e3b9b1823e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-997d42bb-b84a-5f7f-8a34-f6035e4d200f" :answer/id #uuid "997d42bb-b84a-5f7f-8a34-f6035e4d200f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2224ac84-3f36-51f0-b57f-7543c32e2f98" :answer/id #uuid "2224ac84-3f36-51f0-b57f-7543c32e2f98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121010 :tutorial/content "If a curve has a curvature of zero at some point, the radius of curvature at that point is defined to be infinite.\n\nIt follows from the definition that a straight line has zero curvature at all of its points. Conversely, if a curve has zero curvature at any point, it must be contained on a straight line.\n\nSuppose now that we want to find the radius of curvature for the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t^2, \\: 1 - t^2, \\: 1+ t^2 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nat an arbitrary point where $t> 0.$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we used the fact that $\\sqrt{t^2}=t$ since $t>0.$\n\nNext, we find the unit tangent and its derivative for $t>0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}t}\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(t) & =⟨0,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}t}{0}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, despite its \"parabolic\" appearance, the given curve is contained on a straight line!\n\n**Note:** Often, the unit tangent vector of a curve $\\mathbf T(t)$ may look a bit complicated. In such cases, the following formula involving a cross product may be useful. It says that the radius of curvature of a twice-differentiable curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nR(t)=  \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} =\\frac{{{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}^3}}}{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right) \\times \\mathbf r''\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120034 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
